<Portfolio title="Godot development harness" description="The development system behind A Household Exorcism: runtime observation, agent playtesting, regression checks, and human review."><section class="page-hero"><p class="eyebrow">Development systems / Godot</p><h1>Close the loop<br/>on the running game.</h1><p class="lead">A development harness shaped by production: tools for agents to act, observe, and verify, supported by written context and human judgment.</p><p class="tag">Open-source release in development</p></section>
<section class="section prose-grid"><h2>Built out of<br/>a real release.</h2><div><p>A Household Exorcism needed more than generated code. It needed repeatable checks, visible evidence, clear handoffs, and a way to distinguish a passing assertion from a good player experience.</p><p>The harness brings those concerns together around a Godot project. The current implementation is tied to the game that shaped it; a broader open-source release is in development.</p><a class="text-link" href="/games/a-household-exorcism/">See the shipped game →</a></div></section>
<section class="section prose-grid"><h2>What exists today</h2><div><h3>Runtime inspection and observation</h3><p>The underlying bridge provides access to runtime information, scene trees, properties, and screenshots. The scored player surface is narrower: it exposes player-visible observation and controlled input rather than unrestricted developer state.</p><h3>Agent-driven play and regression checks</h3><p>The player tool supports movement, clicks, keys, typed phrases, and bounded scripted sequences. Observations produce numbered screenshots and visible-text transcripts. Delivery acknowledgments explicitly leave the game’s response to be verified.</p><h3>Evidence tied to the build</h3><p>Certification tooling checks exported artifacts, fingerprints the relevant code, and records run receipts. Debug observation belongs in a separate build from the player release. Isolated run directories keep evidence attributable.</p><h3>Context, handoffs, and review</h3><p>Repository conventions, written decisions, bounded implementation slices, and independent review give agents a shared working contract. Human playtests remain a separate check on pacing, discovery, and feel.</p></div></section>
<section class="section method"><div><p class="eyebrow">The working loop</p><h2>Observe before<br/>calling it done.</h2><p>Automated checks answer specific questions. A screenshot can show what was rendered. A transcript can establish what input was sent. A human playtest can reveal that a technically correct interaction is still awkward.</p><p>Keeping those claims separate makes the evidence useful.</p></div><div class="terminal"><div class="terminal-head">PROCESS / IMPLEMENTED PRINCIPLES</div><ol class="workflow"><li><div><strong>Scope the change</strong><span>Write the expectation and assign ownership.</span></div></li><li><div><strong>Exercise the artifact</strong><span>Use controlled input against the running build.</span></div></li><li><div><strong>Capture the result</strong><span>Record visible evidence and build identity.</span></div></li><li><div><strong>Review and play</strong><span>Reconcile the evidence; judge the experience.</span></div></li></ol></div></section>
<section class="section prose-grid"><h2>Where it started</h2><div><p>Godot Runtime Bridge was an early step toward direct agent access to a running game. The broader harness adds the production process around that access: scope, tests, evidence, and review.</p><a class="text-link" href="/experiments/">Explore GRB and the earlier experiments →</a></div></section></Portfolio>
